From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the pthread provider, without which bsd_init panics

This is the first patch in the series that is a boot-stopper rather than a latent
defect, and the reason it was not obvious is that it does not produce a link
error. It produces a reboot.

`bsd/conf/files:370-375` lists six pthread files, all `standard`, i.e. compiled
for every board with no gate:

```
bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c             standard
bsd/pthread/sweet_pthread_init.c        standard
bsd/pthread/sweet_pthread_support.c     standard
bsd/pthread/sweet_pthread_synch.c       standard
bsd/pthread/pthread_priority.c          standard
bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c         standard
```

The last three are the kernel-side skeleton and they are already compiled on
milanf. The middle three are the *other* half - the shim that compiles in the
vendored libpthread kext - and on milanf their entire body sits inside
`#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)`, so all three are empty translation units.

## The chain

`bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c:614`:

```c
pthread_functions_t pthread_functions = NULL;
```

There is exactly one writer of that variable, `pthread_kext_register()` at
`:623-636`, and its only caller is the provider itself - the vendored
libpthread-514.100.2 `kern/kern_init.c`, compiled into `sweet_pthread_init.c` by
the `#include` on its line 4. The provider is entered through
`sweet_pthread_start(NULL, NULL)` on line 19 of that file, which runs from a
startup hook registered on line 25:

```c
STARTUP(EARLY_BOOT, STARTUP_RANK_SECOND, sweet_pthread_register);
```

So the table is filled during early boot on sweet, and on milanf nothing is
registered, nothing runs, and it stays NULL.

Then `bsd/kern/bsd_init.c:743`, with no guard of any kind around it:

```c
        pthread_init();
```

and `pthread_shims.c:314-319`:

```c
void
pthread_init(void)
{
        if (!pthread_functions) {
                panic("pthread kernel extension not loaded (function table is NULL).");
        }
```

A milanf kernel therefore links clean, boots, reaches `bsd_init`, and panics -
before `pshm_cache_init`, before the network stack, long before userspace. The
build log is silent because there is nothing missing to link: the symbol exists.
It is a NULL that the same file defines and the same file checks.

This also explains the shape of the worklist: an inventory that only looks for
*undefined* references would have swept past all four of these files, because
none of them is missing. What is missing is a registration that happens on one
board and not the other.

## Why the header is in the same patch

`sweet_pthread_init.c:7` asserts the provider's layout against the kernel's
opaque storage:

```c
_Static_assert(sizeof(struct ksyn_waitq_element) == 56,
    "update SWEET ksyn storage in sys/pthread_internal.h");
```

and `bsd/sys/pthread_internal.h:35-46` supplies 56 only for sweet, 48 otherwise:

```c
struct ksyn_waitq_element {
#if __LP64__
        #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
        /* libpthread-514.100.2 includes the trailing kwe_uth pointer. */
        char opaque[56];
#else
        char opaque[48];
#endif
```

So the trio cannot move alone: widen the three TUs and the assert fires at
compile time. And the header cannot move alone either - it would grow an opaque
box in every uthread by 8 bytes for no consumer.

The box is only ever held by address. `bsd/sys/user.h:163` embeds it as
`struct ksyn_waitq_element uus_kwe` in the saved user state, and
`pthread_shims.c:292` recovers the enclosing uthread with

```c
th = get_machthread(__container_of(kwe, struct uthread, uu_save.uus_kwe));
```

so the address is identical either way; what the extra 8 bytes buy is the room
the provider writes into past the 48-byte end, where its trailing `kwe_uth`
pointer lives. `struct user` is kernel-private, so the size change is not an ABI
change.

Four files, one unit, one reason each.

## Staging was already widened, in the right order

`xnu/patches/0019-stage-libpthread.sh` fetches libpthread-514.100.2 at the pinned
commit `a6c70ca0e544347a0377babf748f831577ec24f9` and copies `kern/`, `private/`
and `include/` into `bsd/pthread/sweet_libpthread/`. Its board selection is

```sh
case "${MACHINE_CONFIG:-}" in
SWEET|MILANF) ;;
*) exit 0 ;;
esac
```

which commit `8a9ed6b` ("stage the port's sources for MILANF too") had already
done, deliberately ahead of this gate. The order matters: the `#include
"sweet_libpthread/kern/kern_init.c"` on line 4 resolves only because the staging
script copies the directory for MILANF. Had the gate been widened first, the
morning build would have died on a missing include instead of the panic this
patch removes - same failure, one layer earlier and much easier to file under
"vendored source problem" and set aside.

## What is deliberately not here

`pthread_shims.c`, `pthread_priority.c` and `pthread_workqueue.c` are ungated and
already compiled on milanf. They are also the reason the panic is not merely one
bad line: `pthread_workqueue.c` dereferences `pthread_functions` at `:1051`,
`:1062`, `:1159`, `:3380`, `:3618` and `:4658`, against table slots the provider
fills (`workq_create_threadstack`, `workq_destroy_threadstack`,
`workq_setup_thread`, `workq_markfree_threadstack`, `workq_handle_stack_events`,
`kevent_workq_internal`). The NULL table gates the whole workqueue path, so
removing the panic without supplying the table would just move the fault a few
thousand instructions later, from a named panic to an unnamable one.

## Verification, and its limit

The provider source is not in the build artifacts on purpose - the staging script
says so in a comment, "no downloaded sources in git" - so `sweet_libpthread/` is
absent from `ci-art3` and from `ci-tail`, and this patch cannot be compile-checked
off-device. That is a real limit and it is stated rather than papered over: the
`_Static_assert` above is the exact thing a local compile would have confirmed,
and it is the reason the header is in the patch instead of a follow-up.

What was verified: the three `.c` gates and their three `#endif` comments, the
tab-indented header gate, one occurrence each (13 replacements across 4 files),
CR=0 in all four sources and the patch, and 0086-0101 applying clean in order.
Link and boot unverified; CI and the device are the morning's business.

